+# Specialty-Filtered Veterinarian Lookups Are Not Cached
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The two unfiltered veterinarian lookups on `VetRepository` are annotated `@Cacheable("vets")`. The `vets` cache is created by `CacheConfiguration` through the JCache API with a `MutableConfiguration` that sets statistics on and nothing else; the provider is Caffeine, configured nowhere. The cache therefore has no size limit and no eviction policy. That was tolerable while every cached key came from the application itself: the no-argument lookup has one key, and the paged lookup has one key per page number of a table with a handful of rows.
+
+`REQ-VET-004` adds a lookup taking a caller-supplied specialty name. Carrying the existing annotation onto it — the obvious move, and the one a later reader will assume was an oversight — makes the cache key an unauthenticated caller's string. Every distinct value of `?specialty=` mints a permanent entry holding a result set. The application has no authentication ([system-design.md § Security Context](../system-design.md#security-context)), so the key space is open to anyone who can reach the route.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Annotate the filtered lookups `@Cacheable("vets")` like their unfiltered siblings.** Rejected: unbounded, never-evicted cache growth driven by unauthenticated request text. Consistency of annotation is not worth a memory-exhaustion path.
+2. **Bound the `vets` cache first, then cache the filtered lookups.** Rejected here, not in principle. Sizing and eviction for this cache is an open question of its own ([system-design.md § Open Questions](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey), item 5), which asks whether vet data changes out of band at all. Settling it inside a filtering slice would decide the caching question by side effect.
+3. **Leave the filtered lookups uncached** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+Lookups taking caller-supplied text are not cached. The filtered veterinarian lookups go to the database on every request. The two unfiltered lookups keep `@Cacheable("vets")` unchanged.
+
+The rule generalizes past this slice: a cache whose key space is not bounded by the application does not get a `@Cacheable` annotation until the cache carries a size limit and an eviction policy.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The filtered directory reads the database on every request. The vet tables are small and indexed on `specialties.name`; the cost is a bounded query, not a scan.
+- Cache behavior now differs between two neighboring methods on one repository interface. The difference is deliberate and must survive review, which is why it is recorded here rather than left to a comment.
+- Bounding the `vets` cache remains open. If it is bounded later, caching the filtered lookups becomes available and this ADR is revisited rather than silently overridden.
+- The unfiltered lookups keep the stale-data exposure they already have. Nothing here changes it.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003, REQ-VET-004
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Caching](../system-design.md#caching) — the rule in its current-state form
+- [ADR: Veterinarian Search Stays Out of Scope; Filtering the Directory by Specialty Does Not](2026-08-26-non-goal-vet-search-narrowed-to-allow-specialty-filter.md) — the product decision this serves
