+Matching that must disregard letter case is stated in the query itself, not left to the column's type or the vendor's collation. The specialty-filtered veterinarian lookups serving `REQ-VET-004` are the case in point: `VetRepository.findDistinctBySpecialtiesNameIgnoreCase` carries the fold in its derived name, so `specialties.name` compares case-insensitively on all three vendors whatever each schema declares the column to be. A query that leaves the fold to the database instead matches differently per vendor — `OwnerRepository.findByLastNameStartingWith` names no fold, and owner search is case-sensitive on PostgreSQL while H2 and MySQL match case-insensitively ([Known Defects](#known-defects)).
+
+### Caching
+
+One cache exists, `vets`, created by `CacheConfiguration` and backed by Caffeine. It carries no size limit and no eviction policy.
+
+A repository method whose arguments come from caller-supplied text is not annotated `@Cacheable` while the cache it would write to is unbounded ([ADR](adr/2026-08-26-uncached-specialty-filtered-vet-lookups.md)). The specialty-filtered veterinarian lookups serving `REQ-VET-004` are the case in point: they read the database on every request, while the unfiltered lookups beside them on the same interface stay cached. Bounding the `vets` cache is [an open question](#open-questions-from-the-survey).
+
+### View Rendering
+
+A query parameter that a paging link must carry forward is written through Thymeleaf's link-expression parameter syntax, which URL-encodes the value. The value's absence is expressed by choosing a different link expression, not by passing `null`: a link expression given a `null` parameter still writes the name, so `@{/vets.html(page=${i}, specialty=${specialty})}` renders `?page=2&specialty=` on an unfiltered listing. `vets/vetList.html` therefore selects between a specialty-carrying expression and a specialty-free one on whether the request named a specialty, keeping the unfiltered directory's links byte-for-byte as they were before filtering existed.
+
 ## Dependency Policy
 
 Minimize external dependencies. Every dependency is an attack surface and a maintenance burden.
